 "last_activity": "2014-07-22T19:37:21Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "hPNFb7TcEgUJ",
   "from": "Peter Li",
   "author": "839dfa09184985a5",
   "date": "2014-07-21T21:16:35Z",
   "subject": "Running Chains in Parallel",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">For the ensemble samplers, I want to run 4 chains in parallel using something like<div><br></div><div>for i in {1..4}</div><div>do</div><div>model stuff</div><div>done</div><div><br></div><div>and then </div>\r\n<div><br></div><div>bin/print samples*.csv</div><div><br></div><div>But I&#39;ve noticed that when I run it like this the warmup/sample times are a lot slower and more variable than if I were to run the chains sequentially [which is slower in the sense that it would take longer than running them in parallel]. </div>\r\n<div><br></div><div>So should I be running them in parallel or not? And if I do run them in parallel, is it ok to use the warmup/sample times or should we just not bother with using the warmup time as a very rough estimate of time to convergence?</div>\r\n</div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "kCmN01AiOJIJ",
   "from": "Michael Betancourt",
   "author": "a5e83218c834e013",
   "date": "2014-07-21T22:06:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-dev] Running Chains in Parallel",
   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; For the ensemble samplers, I want to run 4 chains in parallel using something like\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; for i in {1..4}\r<br>&gt; do\r<br>&gt; model stuff\r<br>&gt; done\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; and then \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; bin/print samples*.csv\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; But I&#39;ve noticed that when I run it like this the warmup/sample times are a lot slower and more variable than if I were to run the chains sequentially [which is slower in the sense that it would take longer than running them in parallel]. \r<br>\r<br>&gt; So should I be running them in parallel or not? And if I do run them in parallel, is it ok to use the warmup/sample times or should we just not bother with using the warmup time as a very rough estimate of time to convergence?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "This is all internal to Stan?\r<br>\r<br>I advocate saving the warmup times for analysis use but not making any claim that they’re actually representative of convergence time.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "RZtoz1T3iqwJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2014-07-21T23:05:19Z",
   "subject": "Re: Running Chains in Parallel",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Monday, July 21, 2014 5:16:35 PM UTC-4, Peter Li wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>But I&#39;ve noticed that when I run it like this the warmup/sample times are a lot slower and more variable than if I were to run the chains sequentially [which is slower in the sense that it would take longer than running them in parallel]. </div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>Unless you choke the machine, I don&#39;t see how running the chains in parallel would be any different (assuming same seeds, different chain_id, etc.)<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
