    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div><br>\r\n&gt; For the ensemble samplers, I want to run 4 chains in parallel using something like<br>\r\n&gt;<br>\r\n&gt; for i in {1..4}<br>\r\n&gt; do<br>\r\n&gt; model stuff<br>\r\n&gt; done<br>\r\n&gt;<br>\r\n&gt; and then<br>\r\n&gt;<br>\r\n&gt; bin/print samples*.csv<br>\r\n&gt;<br>\r\n&gt; But I&#39;ve noticed that when I run it like this the warmup/sample times are a lot slower and more variable than if I were to run the chains sequentially [which is slower in the sense that it would take longer than running them in parallel].<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n&gt; So should I be running them in parallel or not? And if I do run them in parallel, is it ok to use the warmup/sample times or should we just not bother with using the warmup time as a very rough estimate of time to convergence?<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n</div></div>This is all internal to Stan?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI advocate saving the warmup times for analysis use but not making any claim that they&#39;re actually representative of convergence time.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
