     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>On Monday, July 21, 2014 5:16:35 PM UTC-4, Peter Li wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>But I&#39;ve noticed that when I run it like this the warmup/sample times are a lot slower and more variable than if I were to run the chains sequentially [which is slower in the sense that it would take longer than running them in parallel]. </div>\r\n\r\n</div></blockquote></div><div><br>Unless you choke the machine, I don&#39;t see how running the chains in parallel would be any different (assuming same seeds, different chain_id, etc.)<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br>\r\n<br>Ben<br><br></font></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
