  {
   "id": "2lE47QmoMpQJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2014-07-22T19:37:21Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-dev] Re: Running Chains in Parallel",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "It&#39;s easy to choke a machine when you&#39;re doing unbuffered\r<br>I/O, because it&#39;s basically sequential.  \r<br>\r<br>Or when you run memory-intensive processes, because\r<br>the memory bus capacities aren&#39;t nearly as high as the CPU capacities.\r<br>\r<br>And then there&#39;s paging issues.  If you hit four processes and\r<br>only have four cores, you may run into process-switching issues\r<br>with programs other than what you&#39;re running.\r<br>\r<br>Safest thing to do is run one process w/o anything else CPU or\r<br>memory or network-intensive running (no e-mail, no browsers, etc.)\r<br>\r<br>We really need to get those servers we can dedicate to testing!\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
